mentoring is described in the literature  as an essential component to career  success productivity and satisfaction  hello and welcome everyone to today's  workshop on project-based bioinformatics  and data science training for faculty  and mentors  this workshop is for faculty seeking to  examine share and strengthen their own  mentoring practices  faculty will learn evidence-based  approaches to research and conceptual  and practical knowledge about various  specialization tracks which include  precision medicine infectious diseases  neurosciences oncology space omics  agriculture and ai based drug discovery  so before we begin with today's workshop  let me introduce us  we are a us based bioinformatics company  who is working with multiple academic  and commercial collaborators to develop  easy to use analytical tools and our  mission is to make bioinformatics more  accessible  the training has been completed by over  15 000 participants from 162 countries  in over 300 workshops and due to this  fast growth our team is working with  local and regional coordinators that are  helping refine the local program  logistics and leverage our online  training resources adapting them to the  needs of students and researchers around  the world  i am honored to introduce the founders  and team members of the company  dr alfred tauber comes from a medical  background with an expertise in oncology  immunology and biochemistry of  information and one of his major  projects is the establishment of  troubled bioinformatics research center  which has been at the core of what we do  dr lloyd brodsky is the director of  tobal bioinformatics research center and  is an expert in bioinformatics and  biostatistics  our platform is a result of hard work by  some of the people mentioned here on the  right julia panna is an expert  bioinformatician avi tatovsky leads the  development of t bioinfo platform dr  mohit mazumdar is an experienced  bioinformatician with multiple  publications in peer-reviewed journal he  has been developing collaborations with  universities as a mentor and project  lead for omixlogic training and research  fellowship  dr raghavendra lakshmi narayanan is an  accomplished computational biologist  with an expertise in large scale  analysis of multi or mixed data and data  science methods in precision oncology  and protein dna structural  bioinformatics  ms laura murray educational program  coordinator has a background in  microbiology and bioinformatics and her  focus and areas of expertise are in  metagenomics transcriptomics and  genomics  and my name is sunalika ray and i am the  community manager responsible for daily  interaction with thousands of users that  are a part of a bioinformatics community  which is managed by buying biotech  so let's discuss about bioinformatics  today what is bioinformatics it is the  development implementation and usage of  methods and software tools for  understanding biological data  bioinformatics has practical  applications in healthcare agriculture  and mitigating climate change  as this field progresses bioinformatics  poses several challenges for both  students trying to learn it and faculty  trying to teach it  in this presentation i will explain the  current challenges and limitations in  bioinformatics describing how we aim to  address these challenges with the  omixlogic platform describe bloom's  taxonomy and how it can be used in  bioinformatics course work and explore  the omixlogic program and how it  utilizes bloom's taxonomy in structuring  its course pipelines and projects and  finally describes several outcomes of  the omixlogic program  so what are some of the challenges that  the field of bioinformatics is currently  facing  to begin this field is constantly  growing and developing new tech and  software to improve processing time and  power  the data sets that are analyzed are  usually very large and take immense  amounts of computing power and memory  that many personal computers do not have  also despite being publicly available  data are also usually complex to access  and coding knowledge is usually required  to do so  similarly analysis pipelines and  programs require coding knowledge that  many biologists do not have in a  university setting students have the  opportunity to learn the background  information of designing and  implementing a bioinformatics project  but do not receive practical hands-on  experience with the full workflow due to  the limitations mentioned previously  similarly the culture faces challenges  with being able to cover a diverse range  of topics if they are specialized in a  certain area of bioinformatics or if  they are unfamiliar with bioinformatics  in general  for example a professor whose research  focuses on oncogene expression might not  know much about metagenomics of sea  water  to overcome these challenges the  omixlogic platform was developed this  program aims to standardize organize and  systemize bioinformatics training in  order to make these analysis usable for  any biologist or clinician  some problems for standardization  materials for these courses are usually  developed in-house taught sporadically  and are rarely updated  organization because the challenge is to  be is to combine several different  topics together the flow and transitions  are needed to be developed  systemization how can you know that  someone is stuck and needs help or allow  someone to run forward  omixlogic uses standardized modules and  interactive sessions beginning with  mastering terminology and concepts to  implementing these concepts with real  data sets in a project based approach  learning process in order to create a  clear path of skill development  omixlogic employs the bloom's taxonomy  levels of learning this approach starts  with the basics students recall facts  and terminology and are able to repeat  and memorize the material with this  space the learning builds to conceptual  knowledge being able to understand  concepts and apply information to new  situations  the student will then be able to analyze  data by drawing connections and evaluate  when to use certain methods this leads  them to the highest level of learning  the ability to design and create  original work  so how do omix logic resources help in  advancing bioinformatics teaching  omixlogic is a portal for practical and  theoretical learning of bioinformatics  composed of courses designed to take you  through the theoretical knowledge  omixlogic takes a combination of  training modules data analysis tools  curated project data sets and  interactive sessions with mentors to  give a student a clear path the bloom's  taxonomy pyramid  no matter what the user's initial  experience with bioinformatics is  omixlogic is a useful tool for gaining a  baseline knowledge of the theory behind  bioinformatics analysis of large  datasets and introduction to basic  coding languages like r and python are  beginning a data analysis project  these six courses were taken from the  core of mixed logic curriculum which is  available online  beginning with an introduction to the  application of bioinformatics and an  overview of data types the student is  able to learn terminology and factual  knowledge to build upon  from there genomics transcriptomics  metagenomics epigenomics and data  science are introduced  each course starts with important terms  and concepts and then introduces case  studies and pipelines related to each  one this allows the students to gain the  conceptual knowledge in order to begin  their own projects  expanding basic curriculum with projects  bioinformatics methods are best learned  in the context of real case studies  which are prepared by our network of  mentors and collaborators that  illustrate how various bioinformatics  methods and omix data types are used in  current research in medicine  biotechnology agriculture and even  astrobiology  the projects are prepared by taking  publicly available data sets and using  them to explore and understand both the  research challenge and the analysis path  to extract meaningful insights from  complex comics data  the omixlogic platform gives students  options for analyzing a given data set  these datasets and associated  publications can be used in several ways  you can learn bioinformatics without any  coding at all for that in each project  we explain the analysis pipelines on the  user-friendly t by info platform for big  data analysis you can also learn the  scripts behind many of the steps  learning to wrangle clean visualize and  analyze data in popular languages like r  and python in order to create your own  analysis  finally you can hear about the projects  and their research applications in our  mental guided programs where experts  share their experience and support  research projects developed by you  here is an example of the code  independent pipeline  the pipeline builder interface on the t  bioinfo platform is a color coded  pipeline that helps establish a logical  connection between the technical names  of data analysis methods and the logical  steps needed to build the full pipeline  data is put in an input file and then  the analysis can be chosen based off of  the type of research  each analysis is separated into three  colors  blue for pre-processing red for mapping  and green for quantification  each time you click a button a pop-up  will contain additional information  useful for building the pipeline the  ease of the color-coded buttons allows  programs to be run without any coding  knowledge  after using the t-bioinfo platform to  visualize the pipelines and practice  analyzing students are then able to take  their theoretical knowledge  remember and understand levels of dooms  and apply that in a project apply  analyze and evaluate levels of groups  similarly faculty should be able to  assign the omixlogic program to their  students with the understanding that it  will give them holistic and  comprehensive knowledge of  bioinformatics beginning with bytes and  molecules and onto designing their own  question based project  another goal of the project is for  students to be able to read primary  literature and understand and recreate  the methodology  this leads to the ability to encounter  errors apply theoretical knowledge of  why those errors are occurring and find  a solution  omixlogic employs the hybrid approach  combining learning and research to  ensure that students are building off of  the first level of bloom's taxonomy  omixlogic tracks progress with  activities like pipelines coding and  literature reviews  quizzes on topics k terms and important  questions and covers a broad range of  topics like biomedical research  biotechnology and agriculture  then students are able to continue their  knowledge base with the research  assessment  making sure they are able to understand  methods challenges and results as well  as being able to comprehend primary  research literature  they should also be able to ask  questions based on data exploration  bioinformatics combines life science  terms with data science skills which  allows researchers to answer life  science questions with reproducible data  analysis  methods mixed logic research fellows are  able to ask research questions about any  bioinformatics topic leading to a  diverse range of projects from analyzing  affected biological pathways in multiple  sclerosis to brain metastasis due to  small cell lung cancer these students  are able to apply what they have learned  in the coursework work with a group of  professional mentors and submit their  projects on the portal for review  students should then be able to reach  the highest level of bloom's taxonomy  create this is achieved through the  creation of their own analysis project  for example semi-dolina an undergraduate  researcher at bachelor university  istanbul turkey who finished her  omixlogic research training was able to  work on the project involving chronic  lymphocytic leukemia cll a type of blood  cancer that has a very heterogeneous  biological background and diverse  treatment strategies here at first she  elucidated 870 significantly  differentially expressed protein coding  genes that were involved in the  biogenesis and processing of rna  consequently these findings led our  study to investigate non-coding rna  33 long non-coding rnas were found to be  significantly differentially expressed  among two conditions based on  differential gene expression analysis  one final example of a learning outcome  of the omixlogic program  is this project presentation for a  project done in collaboration between  nagochi memorial institute  for medical research and pain biotech  here is our team at pine biotech who  were involved with the project dr mohit  mazumdak dr harpreet kaur as well as our  collaborators at university of khanna dr  anita gansha and dr benedict tamensa  this poster was on plasmodium falciparum  optical membrane antigen  robbery neck protein 2 binding which is  a critical interaction for plasmodium  falciparum mineral invasion of human  host red blood cells several proposed  malaria vaccine candidates designed to  prevent such binding and limit meteoroid  entry have been studied with variable  success at in-vitro and early clinical  trial stages by performing haplotype  network analysis of genomic sequencing  data from sites with variable infection  rates comparing our data with malaria  gen repository from another region in  ghana and mapping of genomic variants  onto the protein structures we arrive at  preliminary conclusions about local  variation and immunogenic epitope  effecting variance for consideration  in this poster we share preliminary  findings that suggest that such genomic  data can be used to develop a local  strategy for efficacious vaccine design  against plasmodium falciparum blood  stage inspection in ghana and  surrounding regions  omixlogic is proud to collaborate with  diverse programs for example nepi the  national association of idea principal  investigators we also work with over 15  000 students at community colleges like  glendale community college top-tier  universities like georgetown university  as well as 20 universities around the  world now to talk about the programs in

